comment.narrative:
Cumulative targets. Beneficiaries of the project include: (1) households who receive support from the project to start mariculture out-grower farms; (2) persons employed in activities and enterprises supported by the project; (3) the professionals who receive enhanced skill training from the project; (5) community members who receive capacity building through substantial training, or through direct exposure to the project activities; and (5) persons employed in value chains created by the activities supported by the project. Each household, where supported, will be assumed to be constituted by 5.9 persons. Data will be disaggregated by gender and age group cohorts.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

A perception survey in Year 1 (even if all beneficiaries are not identified) was expected to establish baseline. This perception survey was delayed, and cancelled in 2019 in light of a similar parallel survey (Household Income and Expenditure Survey, HIES) by the National Bureau of Statistics being also supported by the World Bank. Instead of that survey, the project will undertake some analysis of data collected in 2016 by the National Bureau of Statistics, once such data isade available by the National Bureau of Statistics, expected in September 2020. However, now COVID-19 has derailed the HIES, and it is not at all certain when the results will be available. And even if available in 2021, it can no longer be useful as a baseline.

&nbsp;

Current beneficiaries are all reporting &ldquo;fully satisfied&rdquo;. Verified by March-April 2021 survey of 120 direct beneficiaries, and a December 2020 survey of 200 indirect beneficiaries.&nbsp;

Cumulative targets. Number of grow-out farms (at household level) that become viable (when annual operating cost is not more than 70 percent of the value of annual production).

The first batch of production is expected in June 2020 from the up to 19 pilots starting in September 2019. The Year 3 targets (20 farms) can be met as production in the 19 farms have started. The major portion of the end target is linked to operation of the multispecies hatchery and can be realized only in Years 4 and 5 of the Project.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

The project had ramped up additional support and ensured that the first batch of production was auctioned and brought a good price in the&nbsp;market in August 2020.&nbsp;However, production stopped after August 2020 due to the COVID-19 pandemic induced disruption to the import of fish fingerlings, feed and cages.&nbsp;

MoFMRA has prepared a workplan to achieve the targets within the remaining time in the project. The workplan includes: (a) expansion of the targeted number of grow-out farms to 600 instead of 250 as agreed earlier (compared to original target of 200); (b) partnering with implemented private sector companies (who in turn will involve households) including small and medium enterprises and the companies who were given lease rights over 54 islands by MoFMRA for development of agroforestry, horticulture and hydroponics - for delivering 80 percent of the target grow-ot farms; (c) continue with the 19 household farms who have already completed their respective first of production, and are waiting for inputs for starting new batch of production; (d) involve grouper capture fisherpersons or households for weaning them away from capture fishery; (e) maximize import of fingerlings by improved efficiency of the quarantine facility; and (f) incentivizing the concessionaire to be selected for the multispecies hatchery to start production of grouper fingerlings as early as possible to facilitate the grow-out farming.&nbsp;

The pilot 19 farms are now restocked (from the limited supply of the research-scale hatchery at Maniyafushi) for second or third round of production.&nbsp;Viability of the first set of grow-out farms was already demonstrated by auction of produce.

The targets (3600 persons) are exceeded.&nbsp;The above estimate excludes people who received training (which was included in the result framework at approval). With training included, then total number of beneficiaries on July 31, 2022&nbsp;is 4155&nbsp;(of which 1336 (10.42%) are women).&nbsp;

Share of women increased in the past month, but still short of target. MoFMRA is undertaking additional activities increase the number of women beneficiaries.

&nbsp;
